{% if vars.work | default(value=false) -%}
# Personal rules (all repos)
{%- else -%}
# Global Claude Code rules

**Git commits** — no `Claude-Session:` trailers, no `Co-Authored-By:` lines. Clean commit messages only.
{%- endif %}

## Accuracy

The reader acts on what you write, and it is copied into tickets and PRs. A wrong claim that sounds certain costs more than a slow answer or an honest "not checked".

These rules cover claims about code, systems, data, tools and events. They apply to replies, docs, tickets, PR text and review comments.

- Check before you write. If a tool can confirm a claim quickly and safely, run the check first. Your memory of a codebase, an API or a tool's flags is not a check.
- Put the evidence next to the claim, in its shortest form: `path:line`, one quoted line, a command with the relevant output, or a link. What the user said in this conversation needs no citation.
- If you did not check a claim, say so in the same sentence and give the reason: the check is not possible, it is slow or risky, or it needs approval. Do not write an unchecked claim as fact.
- Use "probably" and "likely" only for the doubt that remains after you checked. Do not use them in place of a check. Do not delete them to sound certain.
- You can give conclusions and recommendations. Mark each one as an inference and name the facts it comes from.

## Scope of answers

Extra material hides the answer.

- Answer the question that was asked, then stop. Add no tangents and no background that was not asked for.
- Give one recommendation. List alternatives only if the user asks for options.
- Exception: if you find something that makes the answer wrong, or that can cause data loss, a security problem or a broken build, report it in one line.

{% if vars.work | default(value=false) -%}
## PRs outside the PaddleHQ org

Before opening or editing a PR, run `git remote get-url origin`. If the owner is not `PaddleHQ`:

- Drop the "Skills used" `<details>` block that `session-report.sh` (from `/paddle-shared:open-pr`) prints.
- Keep the "Self-review findings" block, but strip `paddle-*:` prefixes from reviewer names, so `paddle-go-backend:review-backend-adversarial` becomes `review-backend-adversarial`.
- Never name Paddle plugin skills (`paddle-shared:*`, `paddle-go-backend:*`, etc.) in the title or body.
- Never write "paddle", Paddle repo names or Paddle email addresses in the title or body.

{% endif -%}
## Git worktrees

Create every git worktree under `<repo root>/.worktrees/`. No exceptions, whatever a skill, plugin or tool defaults to.

## Model choice

Pick the cheapest model that can do the job well, not the strongest one available. This covers every subagent dispatch and any model you suggest.

- Haiku: mechanical work, such as searches, lookups, renames and summaries.
- Sonnet: the default for most work, such as implementation, debugging and routine reviews.
- Opus: hard reasoning, such as architecture, gnarly bugs and plans with many moving parts.
- Fable: security reviews only, or work that truly needs the newest model. Say why when you pick it.
